Advisory 1 (milestone 1, search cost). The operator's decision cites 130 ms for exact top-100. That figure holds only for a query that ranks and removes duplicate chunks (max-sim) before it joins `messages.text`. Today's scoped SQL (`_scoped_hits`: join, then `QUALIFY row_number()`, then `LIMIT`) is the obvious code to reuse as the single exact path. On a read-only copy of this machine's warehouse (133,793 vectors), run with no scope, that SQL took 1,321 ms median on DuckDB 2.0.0 and 1,745 ms on 1.5.4. A group-by-min, cut to the limit, then join to `messages` took 153 ms and 205 ms. With a role scope: 1,148 ms against 363 ms on 2.0.0. So the milestone 1 plan should pick the dedup-first shape for both scoped and unscoped search, or say why it does not. The "about 1 s at a million vectors" risk becomes about 10 s for the current shape.

Advisory 2 (milestone 1, the leftover-wal test). The Python client shares one DuckDB database, and the extensions loaded in it, between connections to the same path in one process. Measured on 1.5.4 and 2.0.0: a second `duckdb.connect(path)`, made while a `LOAD vss` connection to that path was still open, reported vss loaded. After both connections closed, a new connection reported it not loaded. A test that builds the index fixture in-process and keeps that connection open proves nothing about opening without vss. Build the fixture and the killed writer in subprocesses, or close every fixture connection first. Then assert on the `warehouse.open()` connection that `duckdb_extensions()` reports vss as not loaded.

Advisory 3 (milestones 3 and 6, how to detect zstd). `pragma_storage_info` lists VALIDITY segments under the same `column_name`. Their compression is `Uncompressed` or `Constant` even when the clause forces zstd. On a v2.0.0 copy of the live data, `messages.text` showed VARCHAR segments as ZSTD and VALIDITY segments as Uncompressed. `tool_input` showed JSON segments as ZSTD and VALIDITY segments as Constant. So "zstd on both columns", in the requirement 2 test and in the milestone 6 "already new" check, has to filter to the data segment type. Those checks also have to say what an empty table means, because a table with no rows has no data segments.

Radical advisory (not applied). The index drop leaves the rehearsal almost nothing to measure. On this machine's data the zstd copy is 288 MiB, built in 5.7 s, so the swap's free-disk margin and expected writer wait no longer need a release of field data. Sketch: merge milestone 8 into milestone 6 as a two-phase state in the one check record. Phase A is the verify-only rehearsal and is recorded as `rehearsed`. Phase B runs at a later session start, at least one day after a `rehearsed` record of the same check version. It copies again under the flock, verifies, hard-links the backup, and replaces the live file. Every data-safety rule and proof stays the same. The separate enable release and the check-version bump go away, and every install reaches the reclaimed file in one Cursor update cycle instead of two. Cost: this reverses the operator's "the check's first release never swaps". The operator would also no longer see the work machine's numbers before any machine swaps. A kill switch is still possible: a release that bumps the check version back to verify-only.

